# DRAFT GitHub issue for algebraic-solving/msolve (HOLD: needs user approval to file)

Title: Silent mis-parse of parenthesized constant subexpressions (0.10.1)

Body:
msolve 0.10.1 silently mis-parses polynomial input containing parenthesized
subexpressions, producing a wrong Groebner basis with exit code 0.

Minimal reproduction (char 0, 1 variable):
  x
  0
  x-(3+1)
Expected: GB [x-4]. Actual: GB [x+1].

The parser appears to drop the parenthesized group's operator context
(related failure class to #354, silent x/2 mis-parse; PR #320's parser
hardening may cover it — unmerged as of 2026-08-10).

Impact: any pipeline emitting parenthesized .ms input gets silently wrong
verdicts. Workaround we adopted: emit fully expanded monomial sums only,
plus an input round-trip guard through an independent parser.

Found during a large verification campaign (systems with ~100 vars over Q);
happy to provide more cases.

---

# DRAFT issue 2 for algebraic-solving/msolve (HOLD: needs user approval to file)

Title: SIGSEGV on large valid modular input (0.10.1, -g 2, both -t 1 and -t N)

Body:
msolve 0.10.1 segfaults (exit 139, core dumped) on large but well-formed
positive-characteristic input; no output is produced and stderr shows only
the RNG-seed line.

Input class (found during a large verification campaign, mod p):
  * ring: 172 variables, char 105337 (also reproduces at char 105673);
  * 86 generators, fully expanded monomial sums (no parentheses), term
    counts from 2 up to 2,420,711; total ~31M terms, file ~1.05 GB;
  * input validated by an independent strict-grammar parser: all names
    declared/unique, all coefficients in [0,p), no stray bytes.
Invocation: msolve -v 2 -g 2 -t 8 -f FILE.ms -o out    -> SIGSEGV ~34 min
            msolve -v 1 -g 2 -t 1 -f FILE.ms -o out    -> SIGSEGV ~50 min
(-t 1 crash rules out a pure threading race; deterministic at both primes;
 also reproduces on an 81-generator, 628 MB prefix of the same system --
 smallest crashing prefix TBD, bisection in flight.)
Host: 64-core, 991 GB RAM; RSS at crash far below limits (tens of GB).
sha256 of the two full inputs:
  00c9a4b44a49c3bbdbbce5658fe9f5714416e309a7f149d7e492a470733881b6 (p105337)
  (p105673 file crashes identically)
Suspected 32-bit index/size overflow in F4 structures rather than OOM.
Happy to provide the reproducer files (large) or bisect further on request.
